nd what the paper says\n311f135 Outline rev 16: pooled C2 share is 1224 non-null cells, 85.1/86.7%\n442d94e Outline rev 15: frozen B2 kappa-hat at the grid floor read as a bound (Brief rev 13)\n--- a/Outline.md\n+++ b/Outline.md\n-**C2 — Bias is set by curvature × corner distance; independent of d for smooth responses in the fraction feature (with the mechanism qualifier below).** For a response curved in exposure, linear-fit spillover bias ≈ −½g''(1−2p) (+ third-order term); zero at p=½ to second order; for threshold responses with threshold outside the observed support the linear fit sees no spillover and the bias is the whole jump, independent of d in the limit of large d; at small d the support straddles the threshold and the bias is smaller or of opposite sign (`analysis` plan §3: the threshold slope does depend on d; the d-independence holds for smooth responses). Flexible learners do not remove it (trees flat, polynomials explode). The predictor used in the tests is the population-OLS limit on the realised graph and assignment law (closed-form traces for (a), (b), (e), a Binomial mixture for (c), (d), a closed form for (f)); \"exact\" is a statement about the form, not about agreement with the simulation, so the paper calls it **the population-OLS prediction** and reports the agreement separately (rev. 14, `idea-review`, `story-repo`#6); it is not the finite-sample bias and not the second-o\n+**C2 — Bias is set by curvature × corner distance; independent of d for smooth responses in the fraction feature (with the mechanism qualifier below).** For a response curved in exposure, linear-fit spillover bias ≈ −½g''(1−2p) (+ third-order term); zero at p=½ to second order; for threshold responses with threshold outside the observed support the linear fit sees no spillover and the bias is the whole jump, independent of d in the limit of large d; at small d the support straddles the threshold and the bias is smaller or of opposite sign (`analysis` plan §3: the threshold slope does depend on d; the d-independence holds for smooth responses). Flexible learners do not remove it (trees flat, polynomials explode). The predictor used in the tests is the population-OLS limit on the realised graph and assignment law (closed-form traces for (a), (b), (e), a Binomial mixture for (c), (d), a closed form for (f)); \"exact\" is a statement about the form, not about agreement with the simulation, so the paper calls it **the population-OLS prediction** and reports the agreement separately (rev. 14, `idea-review`, `story-repo`#6); it is not the finite-sample bias and not the second-o\n-*What the paper says (rev. 7):* (a) verdict *not supported* by the rule (pooled point gain 0.034 < 0.05), worded as **no evidence of a useful data-computable bias score, not evidence of none**: the intervals [−0.28, 0.34] (leave-one-mechanism-out) and [−0.27, 0.22] (leave-one-family-out) contain both a useful and a useless score, on 6 mechanism groups and 7 families; this goes in the abstract and the C6 text. (b) **Absolute held-out R² of log|bias| beside every gain** (`bias_reference.csv`, 1215 cells): exact-theory prediction 0.987 (a ceiling that uses the true response; labelled so, not a competitor); every data-computable predictor is *below the constant*: S̄ −0.22, corner×curvature −0.10, S_adj (exploratory) −0.06, degree benchmarks −0.26 to −0.27; the Han–Ugander-style CV loss is also below the constant on the Tier A cells (−0.17 on log RMSE, `cv_benchmark.csv`, 84 cells, where the degree benchmarks are +0.31/+0.33). A gain over a benchmark that is itself worse than a constant is not predictive power: S_adj's gain (+0.20, CI [0.10, 0.30]) is stated with its own R² (−0.006/−0.064) and is not worded as predictive. (c) *Reading, labelled interpretation:\n+*What the paper says (rev. 7):* (a\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "aa66e28a-30a7-54b2-a4e9-5a545844eac6", "at": 1795657238.8608825, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 1302